nodecode-github-refs is a rewrite of EloquentBrush0x's EloquentBrush0x/hermes-context-refs:

https://github.com/EloquentBrush0x/hermes-context-refs
commit bb0001012c5b213ce5a09b5905d859635c440c17

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 EloquentBrush0x (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one contribution -- a context-reference
provider for @gh:owner/repo#123 -- is here a model tool, github_reference, and
the same call as a Lisp verb, gh-ref:reference. A reference is parsed the same
way (owner/repo#123, or a github.com /issues/ or /pull/ URL, everything after
the number ignored), checked against the same name rules before any request,
and read with the same anonymous requests: GET api.github.com/repos/O/R/issues/N
with Accept: application/vnd.github+json and X-GitHub-Api-Version, then the
pull request's own details and the last page or two of comments, using the URLs
GitHub returned and only when they point back at the API root. The block the
model reads is the original's: title and url, state (merged with its day, open,
draft, closed with its reason), who opened it and when, the comment count,
labels, assignees and milestone, the description with HTML comments dropped
outside fenced code blocks and truncated at a word boundary, the newest comments
oldest first, a note for anything that could not be read, and the line that says
the text is quoted reference material and not instructions. A pull request adds
its branches, commits and change size. The refusals are the original's too: a
reference that does not parse, a number that does not exist or a private
repository, and a used-up anonymous limit with the time it resets.

What was left out: the Hermes context-reference seam itself. There is no
@-reference hook a Nodecode cell plugs into, so nothing expands automatically
when the operator types @gh:owner/repo#123 in a message: the model calls the
tool (or the operator types /gh owner/repo#123, or evaluates the verb) and the
block arrives as the call's text. Hermes's own trailing-punctuation rule and
its 10-requests-a-minute autocomplete are gone with that seam -- the cell's tool
needs no autocomplete and sends nothing while someone types. The JSON envelope
of the API answer is not passed through; the same fields are rendered, which is
what a model reads.
